A||||7||Kage86GT4SP||Just bought... rear brakes stuck?
Z||000000||Kage86GT4SP||06-19-99||12:08 AM||||I just bought an 86 GT (SE) with a 4sp, ($1200) and more bells and whistles then I've seen in a long time. It has 141K, stereo with (I guess) an adjustable subwoofer, moonroof, etc. It had a console full of receipts, and seems tight as a tick... so to speak.<p>The car hasn't seen much use lately (how many pine needles can a car get, anyway?) and there are spots of mold in the interior... nothing too bad...<p>But the rear brakes seem to be stuck at least partially on.  I have discolored rotors back there, and the trip home had brakes that felt useless.  I have not driven the car much (the ten miles to get it here) but it felt (excepting the brakes) fantastic!<p>Is there something I can look for to address this issue, short of taking the calipers apart?<p>I am amazed at the information this site contains, and I look forward to years of bench racing with you all, and picking your brains so I don't screw it up too much until I can get up to speed.<p>Posts are welcome (kage@pacifier.com) and I look forward to hearing from you!<p>K.
Z||000001||lowCG||06-19-99||12:59 AM||||Is the E-brake on? Do you know that you have to raise the lever,depress the little button,then,while holding the button down,lower the lever?  On the floor doesn't mean off.<br>Otherwise,the calipers may need a clean/rebuild.<br>My first car came with no brake lining in the rear,only metal.Maybe it's a right of passage on these cars.
Z||000002||batboy||06-19-99||09:06 AM||||I've had rear brake problems with both of my Fieros I own. One my first one, a rear caliper stuck partially closed when I was driving in stop and go traffic. I almost burned up a rotor (it got red hot). I replaced the caliper and had the rotor turned plus replaced the pads on both sides (always replace disk brake pads in pairs, if not all four corners. My second batmobile had one rear brake that did not work at all. I took the caliper off thinking that was the problem, but it turned out to be the flexible brake hose was clogged. Those hoses should be replaced more often than most people realize.<p>First, jack the rear of the car up and set it on jack stands (careful where you jack and set the stands, make sure it's on solid frame). Yank the wheels off. Have somebody step on the brake a few times while you watch to make sure the calipers are pinching the pads against the rotors. If they are working fine, then the calipers are ok. If not, they need replaced. Unless you are experienced with rebuilding calipers, don't mess with them, just buy some complete ones at a parts store. Next, disconnect the e-brake cables from the back of the calipers and spray some WD-40 on the levers that operate them. Carefully try to move the levers back and forth by prying on them. It's possible someone left the e-brake on for a long time and rusted these activators partially closed. If they move back and forth, then ok. Inspect everything else, replace the pads, turn the rotors and bleed the brakes if necessary.<p>Now check the front brakes in the same manner (except there's no e-brake to worry about). If everything checks out ok and it still feels like you have mushy brakes, it could be that the master cylinder is going bad. Again, don't mess with rebuilding it unless you know what you're doing, just replace the whole thing as a unit. This requires the entire system to be bled, which is not a bad thing to do. Old brake fluid gets moisture, rust,  and other crud in it over time. Putting fresh fluid into the system is cheap insurance. Good luck.
Z||000003||batboy||06-19-99||09:11 AM||||One more thing. When you put the tires and wheels back on, ALWAYS torque the lug nuts down to the proper specs with a torque wrench, no exceptions. Fiero rotors are kind of thin and are prone to warp if you don't.
Z||000004||Bruce||06-19-99||09:22 AM||||See if the recall work was done, since the e-brake and rear brakes (I think) were faulty. There should be a sticker in the rear lid, indicating the corrections. I think it reads: 86- something, etc. You can look on the net under "Fiero" for the exact recall descriptions.<br>If it weren't for the brake alarm. there would be times that I wouldn't release the e-brake all the way.
Z||000005||Bruce||06-19-99||07:32 PM||||That should read: FRONT deck lid. Also, you can call your local Pontiac dealer, give them the VIN #, and they'll let you know if the recall work was done.
Z||000006||Cooter||06-19-99||09:39 PM||||While you all are talking brakes and stuff, add this bit of info: If your car still wants to pull to one side when you hit the brakes, even after new pads or calipers and stuff, replace the rubber brake hoses that connect to the calipers. They break down inside and will get a partial blockage. Makes the car dart one way when you hit the brakes and the other way when you let them off. Be sure to use a quality replacement hose, not a corner parts store generic one. I ordered mine from Pontiac and the price was reasonible- they must be pretty good from the factory, they lasted 14 years before needing to be replaced.
Z||000007||batboy||06-19-99||10:14 PM||||Cooter is correct about those brake hoses. That happened once on my Suburban and recently I just replaced a rear hose on one of my Fieros that was totally clogged. Actually, since I had to order it, I bought another one for the other side too and replaced them both at the same time. You can easily check to see if your front brake hoses have ever been replace by looking at the mounting clamp in the middle of the hose. If it's welded to the car, it's probably the orginal.
